	"AI Resume Builders in 2026: What They Do, and the 'ATS Score' Myth",
	"A clear-eyed 2026 look at AI resume builders — what ATS systems actually do, why the 'ATS score' vendors show you isn't what employers see, and how Jobscan, Teal, Kickresume, and Rezi really differ. Compiled from official docs and pricing; not an independent test.",
	"<h1>AI Resume Builders in 2026: What They Do, and the \"ATS Score\" Myth</h1>\n<p><span class=\"pill\">📄 AI Resume Tools</span></p>\n<p><em>By AI Tool Stack Editorial · Last updated: 2026-07-28</em></p>\n\n<div class=\"box\"><p><strong>How this was compiled:</strong> This is a <strong>capabilities and pricing comparison built from each vendor's official docs</strong>, not a test we ran. The central claim — that \"ATS scores\" are vendor inventions — is explained from how real applicant-tracking systems work. Prices are approximate mid-2026 figures; confirm on the linked pricing pages.</p></div>\n\n<h2>The number that doesn't exist</h2>\n<p>Open almost any \"best AI resume builder\" page and you'll see a table: <em>Jobscan — ATS score 94%. Teal — 89%.</em> Here is the uncomfortable part: <strong>no employer's applicant-tracking system outputs a 0–100 \"ATS score\" for your resume.</strong> Workday, Greenhouse, Taleo, and Lever parse your resume into fields and match keywords against the job description. They don't grade you. The \"score\" these tools show is <em>their own</em> metric — usually how well your resume's keywords line up with a JD you pasted in. Useful as a checklist, misleading as a verdict.</p>\n\n<h2>What an ATS actually does</h2>\n<p>An applicant-tracking system is a parser and a filter, not a judge. In practice it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extracts</strong> your resume into structured fields (name, employers, skills) — and chokes on tables, columns, and images.</li>\n<li><strong>Matches</strong> those fields against the job's required keywords.</li>\n<li><strong>Surfaces</strong> candidates to a recruiter, who then reads the actual document.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the two things that genuinely help are: (1) a clean, parseable format, and (2) the right keywords present and truthful. An AI builder helps with both — but it cannot manufacture the interview, and it cannot invent experience you don't have.</p>\n\n<h2>The tools, by what they're for</h2>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Built for</th><th>Starting price (approx, mid-2026)</th><th>Official pricing</th></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Teal</strong></td><td>Free resume builder + job-tracker, keyword matching</td><td>Free + Teal+ (~$9/week)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.tealhq.com/pricing\">tealhq.com/pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Jobscan</strong></td><td>Keyword-match against a specific JD</td><td>Free limited scans + paid (~$49.95/mo)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.jobscan.co/pricing\">jobscan.co/pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Kickresume</strong></td><td>Design-led templates, easy export</td><td>Free + Premium (~$12/mo, billed annually)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.kickresume.com/en/pricing/\">kickresume.com/pricing</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Rezi</strong></td><td>AI rewriting with ATS-friendly structure</td><td>Free + Pro (~$29/mo)</td><td><a href=\"https://www.rezi.ai/pricing\">rezi.ai/pricing</a></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><em>Prices are approximate mid-2026 list figures and shift; the linked pages are the source of truth.</em></p>\n\n<h2>What each one is genuinely good at</h2>\n<p><strong>Teal</strong> is the sensible starting point because the free tier is usable: a clean builder, a job tracker, and keyword-matching against a saved description. Pay only if you want the resume-score and bulk features.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Jobscan</strong> does one job narrowly and well — paste a posting, see which required keywords your resume is missing. That's the \"match rate\" you see; treat it as a gap checklist, not a grade. It's most valuable when you're tailoring one resume to many similar roles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Kickresume</strong> wins on design and ease. If your field cares about visual polish (creative, marketing, some founders), its templates export cleanly to PDF. Less of a \"keyword engine,\" more of a \"make it look right\" tool.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rezi</strong> leans on AI rewriting to restructure bullet points into ATS-friendly phrasing. Handy if you struggle to phrase achievements — but the content still has to be yours.</p>\n\n<h2>Three job seekers, three setups</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>New grad, no paid experience:</strong> Use Teal's free builder. Lead with projects, coursework, and skills; run Jobscan's match against each posting. Don't pay yet.</li>\n<li><strong>Mid-career switcher:</strong> Jobscan to re-align keywords toward the target field; Rezi or Kickresume to phrase transferable wins. Budget ~$30–50 for one focused month.</li>\n<li><strong>Senior exec:</strong> Design and brevity matter more than keyword tricks. Kickresume for layout, a human editor for judgment. An AI tool polishes; it doesn't decide what's worth saying.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<div class=\"faq\">\n<details><summary>Will an AI resume builder get me past the ATS?</summary>\n<p>It helps in two real ways: cleaner formatting (so the parser extracts everything) and better keyword coverage (so you match the JD). It does not guarantee an interview — a human still reads the result.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>What's a \"good\" ATS score, then?</summary>\n<p>There isn't a standardized one. Any score you see is the tool's own keyword-match percentage against a job description you provided. Use it to find missing keywords; don't treat 90% as a pass and 70% as a fail.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Free vs paid — when to upgrade?</summary>\n<p>Start free. Pay for one month only when you're in active, high-volume applications and a specific feature (Jobscan's matching, Rezi's rewriting) is clearly saving you time.</p>\n</details>\n<details><summary>Can it write the content for me?</summary>\n<p>It can draft and rephrase, but you must supply real achievements. A tool that invents responsibilities for a job you never held is a lie on your resume, and lies surface in the interview.</p>\n</details>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Teal pricing — <a href=\"https://www.tealhq.com/pricing\">tealhq.com/pricing</a></li>\n<li>Jobscan pricing — <a href=\"https://www.jobscan.co/pricing\">jobscan.co/pricing</a></li>\n<li>Kickresume pricing — <a href=\"https://www.kickresume.com/en/pricing/\">kickresume.com/pricing</a></li>\n<li>Rezi pricing — <a href=\"https://www.rezi.ai/pricing\">rezi.ai/pricing</a></li>\n<li>Background on how ATS parsing works — <a href=\"https://www.jobscan.co/blog/what-is-an-applicant-tracking-system/\">jobscan.co/blog/what-is-an-applicant-tracking-system</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"verdict\"><p><strong>Verdict:</strong> AI resume builders are worth using — for formatting and keyword alignment, not for a magic score. Fix your content first (real, quantified achievements), then use Teal or Jobscan free to align it to each posting, and only pay for a focused month if you're applying heavily. Ignore any \"ATS score\" that isn't tied to a specific job description you pasted in.</p></div>",
