	"5G Technology Explained",
	"A comprehensive guide to 5G technology: how it works, sub-6GHz vs mmWave, real-world speeds, coverage maps, and the evolution toward 5G Advanced.",
	"<h1>5G Technology Explained</h1>\n    <p>5G is the fifth generation of cellular network technology, succeeding 4G LTE. It promises significantly faster speeds, lower latency, and the ability to connect many more devices simultaneously. Since its initial rollout, 5G has evolved and matured, becoming widely available in urban and suburban areas worldwide.</p>\n\n    <h2>How 5G Works</h2>\n    <p>5G uses higher frequency radio waves than previous generations, allowing more data to be transmitted. It employs technologies like massive MIMO (multiple-input multiple-output), beamforming, and network slicing. Massive MIMO uses many antennas to serve multiple users simultaneously, while beamforming focuses signals toward specific devices for better efficiency and range.</p>\n\n    <h2>Sub-6GHz vs mmWave</h2>\n    <p>5G operates in two main frequency ranges. Sub-6GHz (below 6 GHz) offers broader coverage and better building penetration, with speeds about 2-3 times faster than 4G LTE. Millimeter wave (mmWave, 24-47 GHz) provides extremely high speeds (1-4 Gbps) but has limited range and poor penetration, often requiring line-of-sight to the cell tower.</p>\n\n    <h2>Real-World Performance</h2>\n    <p>Real-world 5G performance varies significantly by location, carrier, and frequency band. Typical sub-6GHz 5G speeds range from 100-400 Mbps, while mmWave can deliver 1-3 Gbps under ideal conditions. Latency has dropped to 10-20 milliseconds for sub-6GHz and under 10ms for mmWave, enabling responsive real-time applications.</p>\n\n    <h2>5G Advanced</h2>\n    <p>5G Advanced (3GPP Release 18) represents the next evolution of 5G, bringing improvements in AI-driven network optimization, enhanced positioning accuracy, and better support for extended reality (XR) applications. It introduces reduced capability (RedCap) devices for IoT and improved energy efficiency for both networks and devices.</p>\n\n    <h2>Applications Beyond Smartphones</h2>\n    <p>5G enables applications beyond faster phone connections. Fixed wireless access provides broadband internet to homes without cable or fiber. Industrial 5G powers smart factories with reliable, low-latency connections for robots and sensors. Connected vehicles use 5G for V2X (vehicle-to-everything) communication, and telemedicine benefits from high-quality video consultations and remote surgery capabilities.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>5G continues to evolve and expand its coverage footprint. While not all users experience the theoretical maximum speeds, the technology has brought meaningful improvements to mobile connectivity and enables new applications that were not possible with 4G.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
	"src/content/dev-tools/explainers/tech-17.md",
