	"Password Security: Best Practices for 2026",
	"Learn about password security best practices: password managers, passkeys, multi-factor authentication, and how to create and manage strong passwords in 2026.",
	"<h1>Password Security: Best Practices for 2026</h1>\n    <p>Passwords remain the primary method of authentication for most online services, yet many people still use weak, reused, or compromised passwords. In 2026, the shift toward passwordless authentication is accelerating, but securing your accounts with strong credentials is more important than ever.</p>\n\n    <h2>The Problem with Weak Passwords</h2>\n    <p>Weak passwords are a leading cause of account compromise. Common passwords like 123456, password, and qwerty can be cracked in seconds. Reusing passwords across multiple sites means that one data breach can expose all your accounts. Studies show that over 80% of data breaches involve compromised or weak credentials.</p>\n\n    <h2>Password Managers: Essential Tool</h2>\n    <p>Password managers generate, store, and autofill strong, unique passwords for every site and service. They eliminate the need to remember dozens of passwords while ensuring each one is complex and unique. Popular options include 1Password, Bitwarden, Dashlane, and Apple iCloud Keychain. Most also support secure sharing, breach monitoring, and passkey management.</p>\n\n    <h2>Passkeys: The Passwordless Future</h2>\n    <p>Passkeys are a new authentication standard that replaces passwords with cryptographic key pairs. Your device generates a public-private key pair, with the private key stored securely on your device (protected by biometrics or PIN). Passkeys are phishing-resistant, cannot be guessed or stolen from server breaches, and work across websites and apps. Major platforms including Apple, Google, and Microsoft now support passkeys.</p>\n\n    <h2>Creating a Master Password</h2>\n    <p>If you use a password manager, your master password is the only one you need to remember. It should be long (at least 12 characters, preferably more), unique, and not contain personal information. Consider using a passphrase: a sequence of random words like correct-horse-battery-staple. Enable biometric unlock where available for convenience.</p>\n\n    <h2>Additional Security Measures</h2>\n    <p>Beyond strong passwords, enable multi-factor authentication on all accounts that support it. Use a breach monitoring service to alert you if your credentials appear in known data breaches. Regularly audit your accounts and remove unused services. Avoid security questions with easily discoverable answers, and treat password reset emails with suspicion.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>Good password hygiene is the foundation of personal cybersecurity. Use a password manager, enable passkeys where available, and always use multi-factor authentication. These practices protect you against the vast majority of account compromise attempts.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
	"src/content/dev-tools/rankings/tech-21.md",
