	"Two-Factor Authentication Guide",
	"A complete guide to two-factor authentication: SMS codes, authenticator apps, hardware security keys, and how to set up 2FA for maximum account security.",
	"<h1>Two-Factor Authentication Guide</h1>\n    <p>Two-factor authentication (2FA) adds an essential second layer of security to your online accounts. Even if an attacker obtains your password, they cannot access your account without the second factor. Understanding the different types of 2FA and implementing them correctly dramatically reduces your risk of account compromise.</p>\n\n    <h2>Why 2FA Matters</h2>\n    <p>Passwords alone are vulnerable to phishing, data breaches, and credential stuffing attacks. 2FA requires something you know (your password) plus something you have (a phone, security key, or authenticator app). This makes unauthorized access exponentially more difficult. Accounts without 2FA are over 99% more likely to be compromised than those with it enabled.</p>\n\n    <h2>SMS and Email Codes: Convenient but Less Secure</h2>\n    <p>Receiving codes via SMS or email is the most common form of 2FA, but it is also the least secure. SIM swapping attacks can intercept SMS codes, and email accounts can be compromised. While SMS 2FA is far better than no 2FA, it should be considered a baseline. Upgrade to authenticator apps or hardware keys when possible.</p>\n\n    <h2>Authenticator Apps</h2>\n    <p>Authenticator apps generate time-based one-time passwords (TOTP) that refresh every 30 seconds. These apps work offline and are not vulnerable to SIM swapping. Popular options include Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator, Authy, and 2FAS. Authy offers encrypted cloud backups, while 2FAS provides excellent security with encrypted exports.</p>\n\n    <h2>Hardware Security Keys</h2>\n    <p>Hardware security keys like YubiKey, Google Titan, and Nitrokey provide the highest level of 2FA security. These physical devices connect via USB, NFC, or Bluetooth and use public-key cryptography. They are phishing-resistant because they verify the website domain before authenticating. Hardware keys are recommended for high-value accounts like email, password managers, and financial services.</p>\n\n    <h2>Setting Up 2FA Correctly</h2>\n    <p>When enabling 2FA, save backup codes in a secure location (not just on your phone). Consider using multiple second factors: a hardware key as primary, an authenticator app as backup, and printed backup codes stored safely. Avoid using the same 2FA method for all accounts; diversify your authentication methods for resilience.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>Two-factor authentication is one of the most effective security measures available. Enable it on every account that supports it, and prioritize authenticator apps or hardware keys over SMS codes. The few extra seconds to enter a code could save you from devastating account compromise.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
	"src/content/dev-tools/guides/tech-22.md",
