	"Phishing Protection: How to Spot Scams",
	"Learn to identify and protect against phishing attacks: email phishing, smishing, vishing, spear phishing tactics, and how to verify suspicious messages.",
	"<h1>Phishing Protection: How to Spot Scams</h1>\n    <p>Phishing remains one of the most common and effective cyber attack methods. Attackers use deceptive emails, text messages, and phone calls to trick victims into revealing sensitive information, downloading malware, or transferring money. Understanding how to identify phishing attempts is a critical cybersecurity skill.</p>\n\n    <h2>Email Phishing: Red Flags to Watch For</h2>\n    <p>Phishing emails often exhibit telltale signs: generic greetings instead of your name, urgent or threatening language demanding immediate action, mismatched sender addresses (the display name may be legitimate but the email address is not), poor grammar and spelling, unexpected attachments or links, and requests for personal information. Hover over links to preview the actual destination before clicking.</p>\n\n    <h2>Spear Phishing and Whaling</h2>\n    <p>Spear phishing targets specific individuals with personalized messages crafted from research about the victim. Whaling targets executives and high-profile individuals with sophisticated attacks. These attacks are harder to detect because they use relevant context, such as mentioning colleagues, recent events, or business relationships. Verification through a separate communication channel is essential.</p>\n\n    <h2>Smishing and Vishing</h2>\n    <p>Smishing (SMS phishing) uses text messages with links or phone numbers. Common lures include package delivery notifications, bank alerts, and account verification requests. Vishing (voice phishing) uses phone calls, often with spoofed caller IDs. Attackers may impersonate tech support, bank representatives, or government officials. Remember that legitimate organizations will not call unsolicited asking for passwords or payment information.</p>\n\n    <h2>AI-Enhanced Phishing</h2>\n    <p>Artificial intelligence has made phishing attacks more sophisticated. AI can generate convincing phishing emails without grammatical errors, clone voices for vishing attacks, and create deepfake video for impersonation. AI-powered phishing tools can also automate target research and personalization. This makes vigilance and verification even more critical.</p>\n\n    <h2>Protecting Yourself and Reporting Scams</h2>\n    <p>Use email spam filters, enable multi-factor authentication, and never click links in unsolicited messages. Verify requests through official channels, use a password manager that autofills only on legitimate sites, and report phishing attempts to your email provider, the Anti-Phishing Working Group, or relevant authorities. If you fall victim, act quickly to change passwords and notify affected institutions.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>Phishing attacks continue to evolve, but the fundamentals of protection remain the same: slow down, verify before trusting, and never share sensitive information in response to unsolicited requests. When in doubt, contact the organization directly using known contact information.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
	"src/content/dev-tools/guides/tech-24.md",
