	"Git Version Control: A Complete Guide",
	"A complete guide to Git version control: basic commands, branching strategies, GitHub workflows, collaboration best practices, and Git tips for developers.",
	"<h1>Git Version Control: A Complete Guide</h1>\n    <p>Git is the most widely used version control system in the world, essential for modern software development. It tracks changes to files over time, enables collaboration among developers, and provides safety nets for experimenting with code. Understanding Git is a fundamental skill for every developer.</p>\n\n    <h2>Core Concepts</h2>\n    <p>Git operates on a few key concepts: repositories (repos) store your project files and history; commits are snapshots of changes with descriptive messages; branches allow parallel development on different features or versions; and the staging area lets you prepare commits selectively. Git is distributed, meaning every developer has a complete copy of the repository history on their local machine.</p>\n\n    <h2>Essential Git Commands</h2>\n    <p>Basic Git commands every developer should know: git init (create a new repository), git clone (copy an existing repository), git add (stage changes), git commit (save staged changes with a message), git push (upload commits to a remote), git pull (fetch and merge changes from remote), git branch (list/create branches), git checkout/switch (switch branches), and git merge (combine branches).</p>\n\n    <h2>Branching Strategies</h2>\n    <p>Common branching strategies include: Git Flow (main, develop, feature, release, and hotfix branches for structured development); GitHub Flow (a simpler model with main branch and feature branches, using pull requests for changes); and trunk-based development (short-lived feature branches merged frequently to main). The best strategy depends on team size, release cycle, and project complexity.</p>\n\n    <h2>Pull Requests and Code Review</h2>\n    <p>Pull requests (PRs) are the foundation of collaborative development on platforms like GitHub, GitLab, and Bitbucket. A PR proposes changes from one branch to another, triggering code review, automated testing, and discussion before merging. Best practices include writing clear PR descriptions, keeping PRs focused and manageable in size, addressing review comments constructively, and using automated checks to maintain code quality.</p>\n\n    <h2>Git Best Practices</h2>\n    <p>Write clear, descriptive commit messages that explain why a change was made (not just what changed). Commit frequently with logical units of change. Pull and rebase regularly to minimize merge conflicts. Use .gitignore to exclude generated files and sensitive information. Never commit secrets, credentials, or large binary files. Learn to use interactive rebase to clean up commit history before sharing.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>Git is an indispensable tool for modern software development. Mastering Git's core concepts and workflows enables effective collaboration, protects against mistakes, and provides a complete history of project evolution. Invest time in learning Git properly; it will pay dividends throughout your career.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
