	"Motherboard Guide: Choosing the Right Board",
	"A comprehensive motherboard buying guide covering form factors, chipsets, socket types, VRM quality, and features to consider when building a PC.",
	"<h1>Motherboard Guide: Choosing the Right Board</h1>\n    <p>The motherboard is the backbone of any computer, connecting all components and enabling communication between them. Choosing the right motherboard is essential for system stability, performance, and future upgradeability. This guide covers the key factors to consider when selecting a motherboard.</p>\n\n    <h2>Form Factors: ATX, Micro-ATX, and Mini-ITX</h2>\n    <p>ATX is the standard size, offering the most expansion slots, ports, and features. Micro-ATX is smaller and more affordable while still providing good expandability. Mini-ITX is the smallest form factor, ideal for compact builds but with limited expansion options. Your case choice will determine which form factor you can use.</p>\n\n    <h2>CPU Socket Compatibility</h2>\n    <p>The motherboard's CPU socket must match your processor. Intel's current sockets include LGA 1700 for 12th/13th/14th Gen Core processors and the newer LGA 1851 for upcoming generations. AMD uses AM5 for Ryzen 7000/8000/9000 series. Always verify socket compatibility before purchasing.</p>\n\n    <h2>Chipset Features and Tiers</h2>\n    <p>The chipset determines available features, including PCIe lane count, USB port support, and overclocking capabilities. Intel's chipsets range from entry-level H610 to premium Z790/Z890. AMD's chipsets include A620, B650, and X670E. Higher-end chipsets offer more PCIe 5.0 lanes, faster USB ports, and better overclocking support.</p>\n\n    <h2>VRM Quality and Power Delivery</h2>\n    <p>Voltage Regulator Modules (VRMs) convert power from the power supply into clean, stable voltage for the CPU. Higher-quality VRMs with more phases and better cooling are important for high-end processors and overclocking. Budget boards may struggle with power-hungry CPUs under sustained loads.</p>\n\n    <h2>PCIe Slots and Storage Options</h2>\n    <p>Modern motherboards feature PCIe 5.0 slots for GPUs and NVMe SSDs, offering double the bandwidth of PCIe 4.0. Consider how many M.2 slots you need for SSDs and whether they support PCIe 5.0 speeds. SATA ports are still useful for traditional SSDs and hard drives.</p>\n\n    <h2>Connectivity and I/O</h2>\n    <p>Look for motherboards with sufficient USB ports including USB-C with high-speed support. Built-in WiFi 6E or WiFi 7, Bluetooth 5.3, 2.5Gb Ethernet, and high-quality audio codecs are valuable features. Rear I/O should include enough display outputs if using integrated graphics.</p>\n\n    <div class=\"footer\">\n        <p>A well-chosen motherboard provides a stable foundation for your build and room for future upgrades. Balance features with your budget, and prioritize VRM quality, storage options, and connectivity for long-term satisfaction.</p>\n        <p style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:0.85em;opacity:0.7;\">📊 <span id=\"busuanzi_container_site_pv\">Total visits <span id=\"busuanzi_value_site_pv\"></span></span><span id=\"visitor-ip\"></span></p>\n    </div>\n    <script>fetch('https://api.ipify.org?format=json').then(r=>r.json()).then(d=>{var el=document.getElementById('visitor-ip');if(el&&d.ip){var ips=JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('ip_list')||'[]');if(!ips.includes(d.ip)){ips.push(d.ip);localStorage.setItem('ip_list',JSON.stringify(ips))}el.innerHTML=' | 🖥️ Your IP: '+d.ip+' | Unique IPs: '+ips.length}}).catch(function(){})<\/script>\n    <div id=\"like-box\"></div>\n    <div id=\"related-articles\"></div>",
